nvram1
======

    make nvram

Produces 8192 zero bytes. That is the correct content: nothing in this firmware
reads the file (no nvram dropin, no hypervisor call for it). QEMU only requires
that it exists and is 0x2000 bytes, or it exits.

OBP's real configuration comes from the guest MD instead -- see ../../md/README.

The generator
-------------
    python3 build/nvram/mknvram.py --parse <image>       decode one
    python3 build/nvram/mknvram.py -o nvram1 [--set K=V] generate one

`make nvram` does not call it: python3 is not on every Solaris host, dd is.

It is kept because the format is undocumented and this is the only decoding of
it available. Layout, derived from the OBP sources in src/obp:

    0x0000  nvfixed    0x40      no validity check
    0x0040  nvhash     0x1ee0    4-byte magic, then an append-only record log
    0x1f20  keystore   0x100

    record:  4  hash   big-endian, bit 31 set
             1  crc    simple-crc(data, seed = len)
             2  len    big-endian
             N  data
             a hash of 0xffffffff ends the log

Variable names are not stored, only 32-bit hashes. A string value is
`<count><chars><NUL>`; a boolean is one byte, 0x00 or 0xff.

Not done: making it mean something
----------------------------------
In order of cost. The first two give read-only NVRAM:

  1. Add an nvram dropin to arch/sun4v/niagara/release/dropins.src, so OBP has
     a driver at all.
  2. Implement hypercalls 0xf2 (read) and 0xf3 (write) in the hypervisor.
  3. For persistence, make QEMU write the region back --
     memory_region_init_ram_from_file(..., RAM_SHARED, ...) instead of a rom
     load. Wants an explicit machine option rather than writing to whatever -L
     found.
